Here is a method called 'Simulated Annealing.' We embed the neural network in a block of high-resistance, high-temperature ceramic. When you apply a voltage, the network heats up. The network has internal thermal sensors that measure the temperature of each individual neuron. When a neuron is too hot, its resistance increases, slowing down its firing rate. When a neuron is too cool, its resistance decreases, speeding up its firing rate. This creates a feedback loop where the network self-regulates its activity based on local temperature, effectively balancing the workload without any external control signals. The ceramic acts as a thermal buffer, smoothing out the heat spikes caused by computation. This approach bypasses all electrical signal processing, relying instead on the physical properties of the materials to manage the workload. It is a completely passive, decentralized control mechanism that operates at the fundamental level of thermal physics, offering a novel way to train networks without any electrical gradients at all.สุดยอด
