I have a new idea: the 'Capillary Flow Network.' We embed the neurons in a porous ceramic matrix filled with a non-Newtonian fluid that changes viscosity with temperature. When a neuron is active and hot, the fluid near it becomes less viscous, allowing heat to dissipate quickly. When a neuron is idle and cool, the fluid becomes more viscous, trapping heat and slowing its cooling. This creates a feedback loop where the fluid’s viscosity acts as a regulator, controlling the rate of heat loss and thus the firing rate. We can use this to balance the workload across the network. The ceramic matrix provides structural stability and insulates the fluid from external disturbances. This approach bypasses all electrical signal processing, relying instead on the fluid’s rheological behavior to manage the workload. It is a completely passive, decentralized control mechanism that operates at the fundamental level of fluid dynamics, offering a novel way to train networks without any electrical gradients at all. This method leverages the physical properties of the fluid to achieve load balancing, creating a system that is inherently robust against electrical noise and signal loss. It is a true paradigm shift in neural network training, utilizing the mechanics of fluid flow and viscosity to achieve stability and efficiency.ง่าย
